#{[17592186395003 :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186395005 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395006 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395007 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395008 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395009 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395010 :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $a=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186395010 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(21)^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186395012 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395013 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{21}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395014 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395015 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{21}[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395016 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395017 :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the plane whose parametric equation is $r=sv+tw$, where\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186395017 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nNotice that the plane $r=sv+tw$ is actually the subspace $\\text{Span}{v,w}.$ \nHence, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 5 & -13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 15 & 3 \\\\ 15 & 10 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 34\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186395019 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{35}[\\begin{aligned}13 & 11 & -3 \\\\ 11 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -8\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395020 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{35}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 15 & 3 \\\\ 15 & 10 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 34\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395021 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 15 & 3 \\\\ 15 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -6\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395022 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}13 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 10 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 34\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395023 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 15 & -1 \\\\ 15 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395024 :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the plane whose parametric equation is $r=sv+tw$, where\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186395024 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nNotice that the plane $r=sv+tw$ is actually the subspace $\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}.$ \nHence, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}14 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -16 & 11 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 26 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 26\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186395026 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 26 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 26\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395027 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 26 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 26\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395028 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 26\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395029 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 26 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 26\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395030 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 26 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 26\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395031 :question/problem "$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}{p}_{11} & {p}_{12} & {p}_{13} \\\\ {p}_{21} & {p}_{22} & {p}_{23} \\\\ {p}_{31} & {p}_{32} & {p}_{33}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that the vector $a$ above spans ${S}^{⊥},$ and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of ${p}_{23}+{p}_{31}?$"] [17592186395031 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the orthogonal projection matrix onto ${S}^{⊥}=\\text{Span}\\{a\\}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the orthogonal projection matrix\n\n$$\n{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=a({a}^{T}\\,a)^{-1}{a}^{T},\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={18}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that ${P}_{S}+{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =I-{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}17 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 17 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${p}_{23}+{p}_{31}=(-4)+4=0.$"] [17592186395038 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186395038 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =5.$ Computing $A-5I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186395040 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395041 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395042 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395043 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395044 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395045 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186395045 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+1I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations which is already in row echelon form and has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=1.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186395047 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395048 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395049 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395050 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395051 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395052 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -8\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186395052 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=5.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186395054 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395055 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395056 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395057 :answer/value "5[\\begin{aligned}80 & 60 \\\\ 60 & 45\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395058 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395059 :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=13$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-12,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$"] [17592186395059 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4\\end{aligned}]+(-12)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4\\end{aligned}]+(-12)\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}(13\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4\\end{aligned}]+(-12)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}(13\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]+(-12)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-75 & -300 \\\\ -300 & 100\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186395061 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395062 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -15 \\\\ -15 & 10\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395063 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 14 \\\\ 14 & 18\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395064 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395065 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 11\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395066 :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$"] [17592186395066 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186395068 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186395069 :answer/value "[\\beg