und as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & (-4)^{2} & (-4)^{3} \\\\ -2 & (-2)^{2} & (-2)^{3} \\\\ 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [17592186400821 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & (-4)^{2} & (-4)^{3} \\\\ 10 & (-2)^{2} & (-2)^{3} \\\\ -2 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ -5 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400822 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & (-4)^{2} \\\\ 1 & -2 & (-2)^{2} \\\\ 1 & 0 & {0}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400823 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & (-4)^{3} \\\\ 1 & -2 & (-2)^{3} \\\\ 1 & 0 & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{3}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400824 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & (-4)^{2} & (-4)^{3} \\\\ -2 & (-2)^{2} & (-2)^{3} \\\\ 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400825 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & (-4)^{2} & (-4)^{3} \\\\ 1 & (-2)^{2} & (-2)^{3} \\\\ 1 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400826 :question/problem "A bungee jumping instructor measures how much a bungee rope stretches (in meters) when one end of the rope is attached to the bridge railing while a weight of $x$ kilograms is hung on the other. This data is shown below.\n\n| Weight $(x)$ | **$10$** | **$20$** | **$30$** | **$40$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Stretch $(y)$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, how much will the rope stretch if the instructor uses a $65$-kilogram mass, assuming the trend continues?  \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* \n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{500}[\\begin{aligned}750 & -25 \\\\ -25 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 200\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*"] [17592186400826 :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 20 \\\\ 1 & 30 \\\\ 1 & 40\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2.5\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{500}[\\begin{aligned}750 & -25 \\\\ -25 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 200\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 0.05\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.5+0.05x.$\n\nFinally, to predict how much the rope will stretch if a $65$-kilogram mass is hung from it, we substitute $x=65$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5+0.05(65) \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the rope will stretch $3.75$ meters if a $65$-kilogram mass is hung from it.\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=65$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."] [17592186400839 :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$28$** | **$13$** | **$34$** | **$40$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-8$ | $3$ | $10$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [17592186400839 :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$28$** | **$13$** | **$34$** | **$40$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-8$ | $3$ | $10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 28 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 34 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 40\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [17592186400841 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400842 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 28 \\\\ 3 & 13 \\\\ 4 & 34 \\\\ 5 & 40\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400843 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 28 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 13 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 34 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 40 & 10\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400844 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400845 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 28 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 34 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 40\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400846 :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$22$** | **$25$** | **$28$** | **$34$** | **$40$** |\n| $y$ | $12$ | $14$ | $16$ | $19$ | $24$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [17592186400846 :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$22$** | **$25$** | **$28$** | **$34$** | **$40$** |\n| $y$ | $12$ | $14$ | $16$ | $19$ | $24$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 22 \\\\ 2 & 25 \\\\ 3 & 28 \\\\ 4 & 34 \\\\ 5 & 40\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 19 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [17592186400848 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400849 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 22 \\\\ 2 & 25 \\\\ 3 & 28 \\\\ 4 & 34 \\\\ 5 & 40\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400850 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 19 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400851 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 12 \\\\ 2 & 14 \\\\ 3 & 16 \\\\ 4 & 19 \\\\ 5 & 24\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400852 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 22 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 25 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 28 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 34 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 40\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400853 :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$12$** | **$15$** | **$18$** | **$22$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$-2$** | **$0$** | **$3$** | **$7$** | **$12$** |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [17592186400853 :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$12$** | **$15$** | **$18$** | **$22$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$-2$** | **$0$** | **$3$** | **$7$** | **$12$** |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n\n \nThe coefficients of the hyperplane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 15 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 18 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 22 & 12\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [17592186400855 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 15 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 18 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 22 & 12\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400856 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400857 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 15 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 18 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 22 & 12\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400858 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400859 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400860 :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$30$** | **$20$** | **$10$** | **$5$** | **$2$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$45$** | **$54$** | **$65$** | **$77$** | **$90$** |\n| $y$ | $-10$ | $-4$ | $0$ | $5$ | $12$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [17592186400860 :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$30$** | **$20$** | **$10$** | **$5$** | **$2$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | $45$ | $54$ | $65$ | $77$ | $90$ |\n| $y$ | $-10$ | $-4$ | $0$ | $5$ | $12$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 30 & 45 \\\\ 2 & 20 & 54 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 65 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 77 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 90\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [17592186400862 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 30 & 45 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 20 & 54 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 65 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 77 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 90 & 12\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400863 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 30 & 45 \\\\ 2 & 20 & 54 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 65 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 77 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 90\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400864 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400865 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 30 & 45 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 20 & 54 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 10 & 65 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 & 77 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 90\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400866 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}30 & 45 & -10 \\\\ 20 & 54 & -4 \\\\ 10 & 65 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 77 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 90 & 12\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186400867 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The $A$ is a triangular matrix, we obtain\n\n$$\n|A|=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 4=8\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has identical columns:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- Since $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, its determinant is:\n\n$$\ndet(C)=5\\cdot 4-(-2)\\cdot (-10)=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $|B|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$"] [17592186400874 :question/problem "Let $v$ be a non-zero vector from ${R}^{2}$ and let $A$ be the rotation-scaling matrix given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe description of $Av$ in terms of geometric transformations in the plane is given below. What are the missing entries?\n\n*To compute the vector $Av$, we rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\theta =\\text{_____}$ and then scale the result by a factor of $r=\\text{_____}.$*"] [17592186400874 :question/worked-solution "We can find the scale factor using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{det(A)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(3\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18+18} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent $A$ as a product of a rotation and a scaling matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =6[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the missing entries are $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $r=6.$"] [17592186400881 :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186400881 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )(6-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are always the entries on the main diagonal."] [17592186400902 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =6,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186400904 :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] [17592186400905 :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]$"]}
